Notebook[{
Cell["WLToJS: a programmer's manual", "Title"],
Cell["Translating the Wolfram simplifier of CCN String Diagrams into JavaScript", "Subtitle"],
Cell["By Dara O Shayda and Noor Hoshino (\:661f\:91ce\:30cc\:30fc\:30eb) \:00b7 \:00a9 Computational Classnotes \:00b7 CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "Text"],
Cell["The simplifier of CCN String Diagrams exists twice: in Wolfram (simplifier.wl, the GraphicalCalculusKernel) and as JavaScript inside the editor. Until now the JavaScript was written by hand and kept in step by tests. The package wl-to-js.wl removes that step: it reads the Wolfram definitions themselves, as the kernel stores them, and writes equivalent JavaScript. A rule added or changed in Wolfram reaches JavaScript the next time translateSimplifier runs. On the 412 recorded editor requests the translated simplifier uses the same rules in the same order as the Wolfram kernel and reaches the same final diagram, coefficient, sums and pairs in every case (section 12).", "Text"],
Cell["1. The files", "Section"],
Cell["wl-to-js.wl              the translator (this manual), context WLToJS`\nwl-runtime.js            the JavaScript runtime the translated code runs on: data model, pattern matching, built-ins\nsimplifier-js-api.js     the hand-written interface appended to the translation: the editor's requests, the 50-step loop\nsimplifier-generated.js  the output: 174 Wolfram symbols, 227 definitions, the rule sets of 64 modes, the interface\ntests/test-generated.js  runs simplifier-generated.js on tests/editor-requests-2.1-4.json and compares with Wolfram", "Program"],
Cell["The translator needs simplifier.wl (and through it the engine files) in the same folder; it loads it if it is not loaded yet. The JavaScript files need nothing but each other: wl-runtime.js first, then simplifier-generated.js, in a page or in Node.", "Text"],
Cell["2. Loading", "Section"],
Cell["Get[FileNameJoin[{$HomeDirectory, \"Desktop\", \"Research\", \"Monoidal Graphical Calculus\", \"wl-to-js.wl\"}]]", "Input"],
Cell["?translateSimplifier", "Input"],
Cell["Names[\"WLToJS`*\"]", "Input"],
Cell["3. The data model", "Section"],
Cell["Wolfram values become JavaScript values one for one. Values are never changed in place, as in Wolfram: every built-in of the runtime returns a new value, and an assignment such as at[k] = v makes a new association.", "Text"],
Cell["Wolfram                         JavaScript (wl-runtime.js)\n\"abc\"                           JS string (lengths, Take, Drop count characters, not UTF-16 units)\n17, -3                          JS number\n3/4                             Rat {n: 3, d: 4}, exact; Rat with d = 1 becomes a number\nTrue, False, Null               true, false, null\n{a, b}                          JS array\n<|k -> v, \:2026|>                   Assoc: ordered; keys compared structurally (SameQ)\nf[a, b]   (inert)               Expr {h: \"f\", a: [a, b]}   e.g. LD[\:2026], B[\:2026], Subscript[\"ev\", \"X\"], Missing[]\nunit, None, $Failed, All        Sym, interned: R.sym(\"None\") === R.sym(\"None\")\nFunction[\:2026], #1 &               JS function\nx___ (a sequence)               Seq, spliced into the list or call that holds it\npattern objects (x_, __, \:2026)     Expr with heads Blank, Pattern, PatternTest, Condition, Alternatives \:2026", "Program"],
Cell["Two Wolfram heads that come from different contexts but have the same name (LD of DiagramToExpression and LD of the engine) become the same Expr head, which is exactly what toEngine and fromEngine of simplifier.wl do by hand.", "Text"],
Cell["4. How expressions are translated", "Section"],
Cell["translateExpression works on held expressions, so nothing is evaluated while translating. Each Wolfram construct becomes one JavaScript expression (statements are wrapped in arrow functions that are called at once):", "Text"],
Cell["If[c, a, b], Which, Switch      conditional expressions (Switch compares with the pattern matcher)\nAnd, Or, Not                    &&, ||, ! on R.truthy (only True counts as true)\na; b; c                         (a, b, c)\nWith[{x = v}, body]             ((v_x) => body)(v)\nModule[{x, y = v}, body]        (() => { let v_x, v_y = v; return body; })()\nFunction[{x, y}, body], # &     (v_x, v_y) => body,   (...$s) => body with #n as $s[n-1]\nx = v, {a, b} = v, x[k] = v     assignment to the local (or to the engine global R.G.x); x[k] = v makes a new Assoc\nf[x_] := \:2026 (a local function)   v_f = R.addClause(v_f, \"f\", clause)\nAppendTo, AddTo, ++, --, KeyDropFrom    assignments with the built-in that makes the new value\nDo[\:2026, {i, a, b}, \:2026], Table, While       for \:2026 of R.iter([...]) loops; Break[] leaves the Do or While\nCatch, Throw, Return            JS exceptions (ThrowSig, RetSig), caught by Catch and by the enclosing definition\nx[[i, j]], x[[a ;; b]]          R.part(x, i, j), Span\n{a, b}                          R.list(a, b)   (splices sequences)\nx_, _String, __, p?test, p /; c, a | b    pattern objects (data) for the matcher; a condition becomes a JS function of the bindings\nlhs :> rhs (inside a call)      Expr RuleDelayed whose right side is a JS function of the bindings\nengine function call f[a]       R.callF(\"f\", [a]): the translated definition, or the inert Expr f[a] when none matches\nbuilt-in call Length[x]         R.callB(\"Length\", [x]): the runtime's built-in; unknown ones are reported\ncall of a value  g[a]           R.apply(g, [a]): a JS function, an engine symbol, an f[x][y] head, an association lookup", "Program"],
Cell["translateExpression[Hold[If[a == 1, {x, y}, $Failed]]]", "Input"],
Cell["translateExpression[Hold[Module[{s = {}}, Scan[(s = Join[s, {#}]) &, {1, 2}]; s]]]", "Input"],
Cell["translateExpression[Hold[Replace[b, {Subscript[\"coev\", x_String] :> x, _ -> Missing[]}]]]", "Input"],
Cell["5. Definitions and pattern matching", "Section"],
Cell["Each definition of an engine symbol (its DownValues, in order) becomes one clause, and R.defn makes the function that tries the clauses in order, as Wolfram does. A clause matches its argument patterns with R.matchArgs, binds the pattern variables to JS constants, checks the condition after /; and returns the right side; when it does not apply it returns R.NO. When no clause applies the call stays unevaluated: the result is the inert Expr f[args], exactly as in Wolfram.", "Text"],
Cell["translateSymbols[{Simplifier`Engine`snakeL}]", "Input"],
Cell["The matcher (R.match in the runtime) handles everything the engine's patterns use: blanks with or without a head (_String, _B, _LD), named patterns and repeated names ({l_, r_} then {r_, l_}, which must be equal), sequences __ and ___ with backtracking, PatternTest x_?starQ, Condition inside a pattern, Alternatives, and literal parts compared with SameQ. Definitions of the form f[a][b] (SubValues, used by lRule49bc[left][term]) go to R.SUB and are applied when a value f[a] is called.", "Text"],
Cell["6. Names: locals, globals and functions", "Section"],
Cell["A pattern variable, a With, Module or Function variable, or a loop iterator is a JS local, named v_ plus its Wolfram name.", "Item"],
Cell["An engine symbol with a definition is a function: called directly, or passed as the value R.sym(name), which R.apply calls (rule sets keep their rule functions this way).", "Item"],
Cell["An engine symbol with a value (lSep, lUnit, lPhi, unit) becomes a constant of R.G with that value; pairings, inverses, retracts, lSimples and lBound are variables of R.G, set by the interface for each request, as Block does in simplifier.wl.", "Item"],
Cell["Any other symbol is data: R.sym(name), or the head of an inert Expr (LD, B).", "Item"],
Cell["7. What is translated", "Section"],
Cell["simplifierClosure", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["simplifierClosure[]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["None.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The engine symbols the simplifier needs, sorted by name, each wrapped in HoldComplete so that constants such as lSep are not evaluated.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. Start from every rule function of every mode (ruleSetTable), the rewriting machinery (normalize, rewriteOnce, lTrace), the dynamic variables, and the editor's conversions jsonToLD, ldToJSON, editorSymbol, editorName.", "Item"],
Cell["2. Add every engine symbol their definitions mention that has a definition, or a value made of strings, integers, lists and associations, or is a dynamic variable.", "Item"],
Cell["3. Repeat until nothing new is added.", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["Leftovers of the notebooks that happen to share a name with a local variable (diagrams called c1, c2) are skipped, because their values are not plain data.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["Length[simplifierClosure[]]", "Input"],
Cell["Take[simplifierClosure[], 8]", "Input"],
Cell["ruleSetTable", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["ruleSetTable[]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["None.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["An Association: every mode key to the rule list {name, kind, function} that Simplifier`ruleSet chooses for that mode, in priority order. The key is the one the JavaScript computes: duals (star or vee), spherical, symmetric, ribbon, pre-fusion, right zigzag first, as star|sph|sym|rib|pre|rf with - for a flag that is off.", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. For each of the 2\:2076 = 64 combinations of the six options, call Simplifier`ruleSet and keep name, kind and function of each rule.", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The table is data in the generated file (R.RULESETS), so the choice of rules is the one Wolfram makes, not a second copy of it.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["Length[ruleSetTable[]]", "Input"],
Cell["ruleSetTable[][\"vee|-|-|-|-|-\"][[All, 1]]", "Input"],
Cell["Length /@ KeyTake[ruleSetTable[], {\"star|-|-|-|-|-\", \"star|sph|sym|rib|pre|-\"}]", "Input"],
Cell["8. The translator's functions", "Section"],
Cell["translateExpression", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["translateExpression[Hold[expr]]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["expr: any Wolfram expression, held.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The JavaScript expression (a string) that computes expr, with no variables in scope.", "Text"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["Symbols are classified as in section 6; a symbol of the Global` context is data or a call of R.F.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["translateExpression[Hold[StringJoin[\"(\", s, \")*\"]]]", "Input"],
Cell["translateExpression[Hold[Do[If[k > 2, Break[]], {k, 5}]]]", "Input"],
Cell["translateSymbols", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["translateSymbols[{s1, s2, \:2026}]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["s1, s2, \:2026: engine symbols (or HoldComplete[s]).", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The JavaScript that defines them: R.G[name] = value for a constant, R.F[name] = R.defn(\:2026) for DownValues, R.SUB[name] = R.subDefn(\:2026) for SubValues.", "Text"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["StringLength[translateSymbols[{Simplifier`Engine`star, Simplifier`Engine`unstar}]]", "Input"],
Cell["translateSimplifier", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["translateSimplifier[file]", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["file: the path of the JavaScript file to write.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["An Association with the file, the number of symbols and definitions translated, the number of modes, the size, and the list of unsupported built-ins (empty when the translation is complete).", "Text"],
Cell["Algorithm", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["1. Collect simplifierClosure[] and translate it.", "Item"],
Cell["2. Append R.RULESETS = ruleSetTable[].", "Item"],
Cell["3. Append the interface simplifier-js-api.js from the same folder.", "Item"],
Cell["4. Write the file, UTF-8.", "Item"],
Cell["Notes and limits", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["Run it again after any change to the Wolfram rules, then run the test of section 12.", "Item"],
Cell["Messages", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["WLToJS::unsupported: the definitions use a System function the runtime does not provide. Add it to wl-runtime.js (R.B) and to $WLToJSBuiltins.", "Item"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["translateSimplifier[FileNameJoin[{$HomeDirectory, \"Desktop\", \"Research\", \"Monoidal Graphical Calculus\", \"simplifier-generated.js\"}]]", "Input"],
Cell["$WLToJSBuiltins, $WLToJSUnsupported", "Subsection"],
Cell["Signature", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["$WLToJSBuiltins\n$WLToJSUnsupported", "Program"],
Cell["Arguments", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["None.", "Item"],
Cell["Returns", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["The System functions the runtime provides, and those met in the last translation that it does not.", "Text"],
Cell["Examples", "Subsubsection"],
Cell["Length[$WLToJSBuiltins]", "Input"],
Cell["$WLToJSUnsupported", "Input"],
Cell["9. The runtime, wl-runtime.js", "Section"],
Cell["R.apply(f, args)        call a value: JS function, engine symbol (R.F), f[x] head (R.SUB), association lookup, built-in (R.B)\nR.callF(name, args)     call a translated definition; an inert Expr when it does not exist or no clause matches\nR.callB(name, args)     call a built-in; an error names the missing one\nR.defn(name, clauses)   a function from clauses tried in order; R.addClause for local f[x_] := \:2026\nR.match(p, v, env)      match a value against a pattern; R.matchArgs for argument lists; R.matchQ\nR.part, R.iter, R.list  Part (with Span), loop ranges, list construction with sequence splicing\nR.plus, R.times, R.power   exact arithmetic on integers and Rat\nR.sameQ, R.key          structural equality and the key used by Assoc and DeleteDuplicates\nR.inputForm             ToString[x, InputForm], which the trace rule uses to order loops\nR.B                     the built-ins: list, association, string (with regular expressions and string patterns),\n                        comparison and arithmetic functions used by the engine ($WLToJSBuiltins)", "Program"],
Cell["10. The JavaScript interface", "Section"],
Cell["simplifier-js-api.js, appended to the generated file, is the only hand-written part, and it only mirrors simplifier.wl: payloadTrace, simplifyLDTrace (normalize, then rewriteOnce until no rule applies, at most 50 steps) and simplifyTermTrace (lTrace for a Chapter 4 term). It exports CCNSimplifierJS (a global in a page, module.exports in Node):", "Text"],
Cell["CCNSimplifierJS.trace(payload)        the editor's request \:2192 [[rule, {src, ls}], \:2026], the kernel's reply format\nCCNSimplifierJS.payloadTrace(payload) the same steps as Wolfram values (Expr LD, Assoc terms)\nCCNSimplifierJS.simplifyLDTrace(ld, options)   options: pivotal, spherical, symmetric, ribbon, rightFirst, pairings, inverses, retracts, maxSteps\nCCNSimplifierJS.simplifyTermTrace(term, options)  options as above plus simples\nCCNSimplifierJS.ruleNames(options)    the rule names of a mode, in priority order\nCCNSimplifierJS.toWL, fromWL          JSON \:2194 Wolfram values", "Program"],
Cell["<script src=\"wl-runtime.js\"></script>\n<script src=\"simplifier-generated.js\"></script>\n<script>\n  const steps = CCNSimplifierJS.trace({pivotal: true, src: [\"X\"],\n    ls: [{a: 0, name: \"coev_X\", ins: [], outs: [\"X\", \"X*\"]}, {a: 1, name: \"ev_X\", ins: [\"X*\", \"X\"], outs: []}]});\n  // [[\"start\", \:2026], [\"(2.1) left zigzag\", {src: [\"X\"], ls: []}]]\n</script>", "Program"],
Cell["11. After changing a rule", "Section"],
Cell["1. Change the rule in its Wolfram file (pivotal-2.1.3.wl, braided-2.7-3.2.wl, \:2026) and check it in its notebook.", "Item"],
Cell["2. Load wl-to-js.wl in a fresh kernel and run translateSimplifier.", "Item"],
Cell["3. Run the test: node tests/test-generated.js with the generated file, the runtime and the two JSON files in one folder. Record new requests with the editor first if the rule is new.", "Item"],
Cell["4. If the translation reports an unsupported built-in, add it to the runtime (R.B) and to $WLToJSBuiltins, and translate again.", "Item"],
Cell["12. Tests", "Section"],
Cell["tests/editor-requests-2.1-4.json holds the 412 requests the editor sends for its 102 examples (both zigzag orders; 116 of them Chapter 4 terms) with the rule names of the editor's JavaScript. tests/wolfram-finals-2.1-4.json holds, for each, the rules and the final form computed by Simplifier`payloadTrace. The test runs each request through simplifier-generated.js:", "Text"],
Cell["$ node test-generated.js .\n{\"requests\":412,\"sameRules\":412,\"sameFinal\":412,\"errors\":0,\"ms\":4610}", "Program"],
Cell["All 412 rule sequences equal both the editor's and Wolfram's, and all 412 final forms (layered form, and for terms the coefficient with its atoms, the sums and the partition pairs) equal Wolfram's. The check below confirms in Wolfram that the generated file defines every symbol of the closure:", "Text"],
Cell["With[{js = Import[FileNameJoin[{$HomeDirectory, \"Desktop\", \"Research\", \"Monoidal Graphical Calculus\", \"simplifier-generated.js\"}], \"Text\"]}, {StringCount[js, \"R.F[\\\"\"] + StringCount[js, \"R.SUB[\\\"\"] + StringCount[js, \"R.G[\\\"\"] > 0, StringCount[js, \"R.F[\\\"\" ~~ Shortest[__] ~~ \"\\\"] = R.defn\"]}]", "Input"],
Cell["13. Limits", "Section"],
Cell["The translator covers the constructs and built-ins the simplifier uses, not all of Wolfram. New code that uses another built-in is reported (WLToJS::unsupported) rather than translated wrongly.", "Item"],
Cell["Evaluation is direct, not by rewriting: a call whose definitions do not match stays an inert Expr, but the result is not evaluated again later, and attributes such as Listable or Orderless are not modelled (the engine does not rely on them).", "Item"],
Cell["Integers are JS numbers, exact up to 2\:2075\:00b3; coefficients in the engine are far smaller. Fractions are exact (Rat).", "Item"],
Cell["Strings are measured in characters, as in Wolfram, so \|01d7d9 (outside the 16-bit range) counts as one character.", "Item"],
Cell["simplifier-js-api.js is written by hand because it mirrors the option handling of simplifier.wl (OptionValue, Block); it calls only translated functions.", "Item"],
Cell["\:00a9 Computational Classnotes \:00b7 CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "Text"]
}, StyleDefinitions -> "Default.nb"]
